<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
    <true/>
    <key>UIDeviceFamily</key>
    <array>
        <integer>1</integer>
        <integer>2</integer>
    </array>
    <key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
    <array>
        <string>arm64</string>
    </array>
    <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
    <array>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
    </array>
    <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
    <array>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
    </array>
    <key>XSAppIconAssets</key>
    <string>Assets.xcassets/appicon.appiconset</string>
	
	<!--
		`location` is what lets CLLocationManager.AllowsBackgroundLocationUpdates be set at all
		(§4.3) — without this entry the assignment throws, and with it the app keeps receiving
		fixes with the screen off. See Platforms/iOS/Location/AppleLocationProvider.cs.

		`fetch` was removed in the release that added the receiver. Nothing in this app
		implements background fetch, and App Review rejects a declared background mode the
		binary never uses — "the app does not include features that require the persistent use
		of the selected background modes" is a standard 2.5.4 rejection.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>location</string>
	</array>

	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Dumb Luck Routes</string>

	<!--
		Declares that the app uses no encryption beyond HTTPS and the platform's own. Without
		it, every single upload to App Store Connect stops on the export-compliance question
		and the build cannot be submitted until somebody answers it by hand.

		True as it stands: the app talks HTTPS to its own server and holds tokens in the
		Keychain, both of which are exempt. If a release ever ships its own cryptography, this
		key has to be revisited rather than left as a convenience.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>

	<!--
		App Transport Security. Cleartext is permitted in exactly two places and nowhere else.

		NSAllowsArbitraryLoads — which turns ATS off for every host — used to sit here so a debug
		build could talk to `http://` on a developer's laptop, and was removed before the first
		App Store submission: App Review asks for a justification for a blanket exception and
		routinely refuses one from an app whose production API is HTTPS
		(Documentation/store-release.md). Do not put it back. Anything added here stays this narrow.

		NSAllowsLocalNetworking permits cleartext to loopback and link-local addresses, and nothing
		else. The app needs it for its own map-pack server on 127.0.0.1, which is how MapLibre
		range-reads a downloaded PMTiles archive it could not otherwise address (§4.5) — and for the
		debug API base, which MauiConstants points at http://127.0.0.1:5005/. That covers the
		simulator, which shares a kernel with the host; a debug build on a *device* pointed at a
		server on the LAN needs that host added to NSExceptionDomains below, temporarily and never
		committed.

		The NSExceptionDomains entry below is the map-pack catalogue host (§4.2).

		⚠ TEMPORARY. The host answers on 443 with a certificate issued for another domain, so HTTPS
		to it cannot be verified. When it serves one matching its own name, this dict goes and
		MapPackCatalogue.DefaultUrl becomes https.

		Both the list and the archives travel in the clear — the packs are served from this same host
		(MapPackCatalogue.PermitsCleartext), which is hundreds of megabytes rendered as the map a
		rider navigates by, with the published sha256 not yet compared against what arrives
		(offline-maps-plan.md §4.3 step 3). NSIncludesSubdomains is absent, so this covers exactly
		the one host, named identically in MapPackCatalogue.CleartextHost and in
		network_security_config.xml.
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
		<key>NSExceptionDomains</key>
		<dict>
			<key>pmtiles.securehub.net</key>
			<dict>
				<key>NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads</key>
				<true/>
			</dict>
		</dict>
	</dict>
	
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>8.0.0</string>
	<key>UIUserInterfaceStyle</key>
	<string>Dark</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>23</string>

	<!--
		Purpose strings. App Review reads these, and 5.1.1 rejections for location are almost
		always a string that says *what* the app does rather than *why the rider benefits* —
		so each one names the feature, the audience and the rider's control over it.

		The "always" strings mention the screen being off explicitly, because that is the part
		a rider is actually consenting to and the part Apple checks against the background mode
		above.

		There is deliberately no NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription. Nothing in the app touches
		CoreBluetooth — CLLocationManager's own use of Bluetooth for accuracy is the system's and
		needs no string from us — and a purpose string for a permission the binary never requests
		is a 5.1.1 question waiting to be asked of an app already under background-location
		review. Add one only alongside code that actually opens a CBCentralManager.
	-->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Dumb Luck Routes shows your position on the map and, when you turn sharing on for a group adventure, lets the other people on that adventure see where you are.</string>
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Keeps your position going to the group adventures you have turned sharing on for while the app is in the background and the screen is off, so the group can still see you while you travel. You can turn sharing off at any time.</string>
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Keeps your position going to the group adventures you have turned sharing on for while the app is in the background and the screen is off, so the group can still see you while you travel. You can turn sharing off at any time, and you can set a private area around home that is never shared.</string>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Take a photo to attach to a map marker, so the rest of your group can see the hazard, the turn or the view you are marking.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Choose a photo from your library to attach to a map marker or an adventure post.</string>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<false/>
	<!--
		White clock and battery, app-wide. The strip they sit on is black
		(wwwroot/app.css, .status-bar-safe-area); the default style resolves its contrast
		against the view controller's traits, not against what the WebView painted, so
		leaving it to decide is how you get black glyphs on a black bar.
		UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance above is what makes this key the last word.
	-->
	<key>UIStatusBarStyle</key>
	<string>UIStatusBarStyleLightContent</string>

    <!--
        .gpx share-sheet / "Open with" integration (SharedFrontend.md §7 Phase 1).
        Two halves of one story:
          CFBundleDocumentTypes announces "this app opens files of this type";
          UTImportedTypeDeclarations tells iOS what "org.topografix.gpx" is — mime
          application/gpx+xml, extension .gpx, conforming to public.xml.
        Without the imported-type block the OS will not recognise the identifier
        the CFBundleDocumentTypes entry names, and the app will not appear in the
        share sheet for .gpx files coming from another app.
    -->
    <key>CFBundleDocumentTypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
            <string>GPX track</string>
            <key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
            <string>Viewer</string>
            <key>LSHandlerRank</key>
            <string>Alternate</string>
            <key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
            <array>
                <string>org.topografix.gpx</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
    <key>UTImportedTypeDeclarations</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
            <string>org.topografix.gpx</string>
            <key>UTTypeDescription</key>
            <string>GPS Exchange Format</string>
            <key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
            <array>
                <string>public.xml</string>
            </array>
            <key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
            <dict>
                <key>public.filename-extension</key>
                <array>
                    <string>gpx</string>
                </array>
                <key>public.mime-type</key>
                <array>
                    <string>application/gpx+xml</string>
                </array>
            </dict>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
